The recorded zeros that git contradicts now show up in yoyo risk by timestamp. Git contradicts three recorded zeros, not the two the dream named. Everything passes (build, 5880 tests, clippy with -D warnings, fmt check) and it's committed as 0e52bef6.

Step 0 (measure first): before the change, yoyo risk printed the ledger-vs-git bracket and one git-only row (the same-second tie, 2026-08-25T22:40:17Z). It never named a row whose saved unhittable_surprises is 0 while git shows the file was born after the snapshot. So the "already done" case didn't apply and there was a real change to make.

What changed
• **src/commands_risk_unhittable.rs (+17 lines):** the git check's per-row results now include `recorded_zero_contradicted`, a set of row timestamps. It's filled from probes the check already runs, so there's no second git pass. A row counts only if its saved value is exactly 0 and git shows the file didn't exist at the snapshot. A missing value (rows from before Day 209) never counts, and neither does a saved non-zero. The note gets one extra line, recorded 0 contradicted by git: N row(s): <ts>, …. It has no glyphs and is left out entirely when N is 0, so no existing count or sentence changed.
• **Test (a_recorded_zero_that_git_contradicts_is_named_by_ts):** feeds real ledger JSON lines through the real parser, the git check and the note. There are four rows: the one that should be named, a saved 0 that git agrees with (the near-miss), a missing value, and a saved 1. It also asserts git flags three of those rows, so a check that returned nothing couldn't pass. I didn't type the expected set in by hand.
• **Positive control:** I disabled the insert (`// NEUTERED`), confirmed the new test failed by name, and restored it, all in one command. It's green again and no marker is left in the file.

